            'full_text' => "The Delhi government on Thursday announced a five-day odd-even road rationing scheme for private cars starting November 10, along with staggered working hours for its offices, after the city's air quality index remained in the very poor category for seven consecutive days.\n\nUnder the scheme, private cars with registration numbers ending in an odd digit will be allowed on the roads on odd dates, and those ending in an even digit on even dates, between 8 am and 8 pm. Electric and CNG vehicles, two-wheelers, vehicles carrying school children, and emergency and essential services will be exempt. Officials said traffic police and civil defence volunteers would be deployed at major intersections to guide motorists.\n\nTo help commuters, the transport department will run 500 additional buses and Delhi Metro will add extra trips during peak hours. Government offices will open in two shifts, at 9 am and 10.30 am, to spread out traffic. Private companies have been advised to allow work from home wherever possible.\n\nThe environment department said construction dust control, road sprinkling and anti-smog guns would be stepped up across pollution hotspots. Officials said the measures would be reviewed after five days based on air quality readings and weather forecasts.",
